  ▸ s238-D7 — RULED THE POLARITY GATE - FIVE REFUSALS THAT RUN IN THE BUILD AND AT THE COMMIT GATE, PROVED BY A MUTATION SELF-TEST ON THE REAL ROWS - (1) a party or link id that does not resolve against the live registers; (2) an untyped link; (3) a judgement text field on a polarity node - its only judgement is a typed link to a knowledge/_rulings.json id; (4) an authored edge file - pairwise edges exist only under a generated path with a content-freshness check; (5) a typed status. Control plus one arm per refusal, driven on the real 30 rows, each arm must go red; migration side_a/side_b/apollo_touch to parties/links carries a receipt. A gate that is not a consumer of every commit is not a gate. (2026-09-02, Dave): Dave at #238: 'Okay this sounds great, so how do we make the risks dissolve' - the five arms presented - 'I'm happy with your recommendations on all', then 'max out the tokens' and 'just test the crap out of it please'.
  ▸ s245-D1 — RULED THE BEHAVIOUR ADDRESS GRAMMAR IS (a) PATH + FRAGMENT, WITH THE FOREIGN REFUSAL. `behaviour.script` in `knowledge/components/<slug>.meta.json` is one of exactly three forms: `null` (a POSITIVE 'the component carries no script'), `knowledge/<path>.js` (a hand-authored source, the component-types.json $behaviour form), or `knowledge/snippets/<Slug>.reference.html#script` (the component's OWN snippet's inline executable <script> element(s), outside AUTO-BEHAVIOUR markers). One grammar shared by the meta, the derived #behaviour-manifest, the receipt and the registry. A `#script` address that names ANOTHER component's snippet is REFUSED as BEHAVIOUR-ADDRESS-FOREIGN on both sides (generator and gate): shared behaviour is a registered partial, never a pointer into another file. Option (b), the KG node-id grammar `snippet:<file>#script`, is REJECTED. (2026-09-03, Dave): Dave at #245, 2026-09-03, given the five L2 questions from `_REVIEW-L2-behaviour-address-2026-09-02-v1.html` § 06 with the conductor's recommendations and the three L3 role-name words, answering all in one line: 'take the recommendations'. Those are his words and they are the ruling. Question 1 as put: 'The address grammar and its constraints. (a) path + fragment - `knowledge/<path>.js` | `knowledge/snippets/<Slug>.reference.html#script`, one grammar with the receipt and the registry, `#script` = the component's OWN snippet's inline script(s) (finding 6); (b) node-id - `snippet:<file>#script`, the KG edge grammar, but a file address has no prefix and the receipt already uses paths. Recommend (a), including the FOREIGN refusal.'
  ▸ s245-D3 — RULED A PROSE `behaviour` VALUE BLOCKS THE GATE FROM THE DAY THE 20 METAS ARE WRITTEN (#245). In the L1/L2 receipt chain (`_validate_receipt.py` step 4b, inherited by `_validate_screen.py` step 0) a markup region whose meta carries a `behaviour` key that is still pre-s234-D5 prose - a string, a list, or an object with no `script` key - is a named FAIL (BEHAVIOUR-PROSE, rc 1), no longer one UNPROVEN line. A meta with NO `behaviour` key stays UNPROVEN (meta:NONE) until rC Q3 and the dataviz migration settle what 'none' means. Population on the day of the ruling: 0 metas go red, because the 20 are typed in the same lane. (2026-09-03, Dave): Dave at #245, 2026-09-03, given the five L2 questions from `_REVIEW-L2-behaviour-address-2026-09-02-v1.html` § 06 with the conductor's recommendations and the three L3 role-name words, answering all in one line: 'take the recommendations'. Those are his words and they are the ruling. Question 3 as put: 'When does a PROSE `behaviour` start blocking? Today: one UNPROVEN line, never red. Recommend: block a meta whose `behaviour` key is still prose on the day the 20 are written (0 would go red then; one flag, ~300 tokens); metas with NO `behaviour` key stay UNPROVEN until rC Q3 and the dataviz migration settle what "none" means.'
